First, two corrections to what I told you. It's 149 methods, not 170. And the list comes back on the first write to each page, not on every write.

**Where they come from:** most aren't LivePage's own. LivePage adds about 17, like flash, alert, save, restore and its draw functions. About 100 are inherited from Page: columns, tabs, markdown, previews, navigation and logs. About 30 come from Item: set, get, walk, emit and so on. So the real problems are that Page has turned into a catch-all class, and the tool lists the whole inheritance chain with empty descriptions instead of just the few methods meant for deltas.

**Compaction:** there's no tool for compacting another agent. There's only `compact_self`, which an agent calls on itself. I've woken the Live page mastermind and asked it to save anything important and then compact. One catch: waking a session that size cost about $8 just to reload its context (it went from $17.20 to $25.20). From now on, big agents should compact before they go to sleep, or simply be retired.

**Fresh audit agent:** mastermind-method-audit (Sonnet, high effort, $12 cap) is now working through:
- each method grouped by the class that defines it, and what the group is for;
- git history: who added each group, when, and whether you asked for it;
- what's actually used, what's dead, and what's internal plumbing an agent should never see;
- why the tool dumps them, and what a minimal Page/List/Item looks like for "cards on a screen".

It's allowed to make one fix: the help will list only methods a class declares for deltas, each with a one-line description. It'll add a test and won't restart Servex. It can't delete or move any methods without your yes.

