content.text:
## Three verbs

Every object has properties and methods. The item tool only needs three verbs:

```js
// GET: read, never logged
item.get("title")
item.get("completion")

// SET: change a property, logged
item.set({ title: "New" })

// CALL: run a method, logged
item.set({ tab: { name: "x" } })
```

A "query" is just a method that only returns a value, like `completion`. It's a get, so nothing is logged.

## The rule for the log

```js
// changes state → log the line
// returns a value → log nothing

// on reload, every logged line
// runs again, in order
```

You don't have to choose between logging properties and logging method calls. The log keeps whatever line was sent, and replay sends it again.

## Instantiating a session

```js
const s = await Item.open(url)
// line 1 says "Session"
// replay builds its tasks,
// and their tasks, and so on

s.completion()
// averages its tasks' scores
```

That already works. I ran it on 5132a480 and got **0.22**: 7 done out of 22.

## Async methods

```js
// the tool awaits the method
const v = await it[name](args)
// returns v to the caller,
// and logs the call if it changed state
```

## Full vs. preview (next to design)

```js
// preview: index only, cheap
const p = Item.preview(url)
// title, icon, completion

// full: replays everything
const s = await Item.open(url)
```

## page_tools.js

Most of its 1,856 lines duplicate `Item.open`. The plan is to keep the item tool, make it these three verbs on top of `Item.open`, and delete the rest that nobody calls. Anything that's actually used gets moved onto a class.

## What next (pick one)

1. **Show me:** a list of every session from the last two weeks with its completion score, so we can see how many need review. It's cheap: one minion.
2. **Fix the tool:** an Opus mastermind rebuilds the item tool as the three verbs, and page_tools.js shrinks. It's big, and needs a restart at the end.

I'd do **1 first**. It's quick, it proves completion works, and it gives you the real numbers before the bigger fix.

## get vs. call

```js
// data: just read it
item.get("title")

// computed: call the method
item.completion()
```

You're right that `get` only reads data. `{"completion": {}}` is the call: a method with no arguments. Classes mark some methods as **queries**, and Session marks `completion`. When you call a query, the tool runs it, sends you the value, and writes nothing. That's exactly what happened when I got 0.22 back.

## Completion is computed

```js
class Session extends Task {
  completion() {
    // averages this.tasks
    // each task does the same
  }
}
```

Yes, that's the right shape. Nothing gets stored and nothing gets rewritten on every change. Opening the session builds its tasks, and the method works the score out live from them. It's already built this way.

## Log the call or the result?

```js
// what's logged today: the call
{ "tab": { "name": "x" } }
// on replay, tab() runs again
```

There's a trade-off either way:
- **Logging the call** keeps the log small and readable, and replay reruns the method. The risk is that side effects run again on every reload.
- **Logging the result** makes replay exact and simple, but the log is bigger and you lose why the change happened.

## How the system handles that today

```js
press() {
  this.set({ pressed: true })
  if (this.replaying) return
  sendMessage() // live only
}
```

It logs the call, and any method that reaches outside the item (sending a message, flashing the screen) skips that part while replaying. Anything random or time-based, like a new id or a timestamp, gets stamped into the line before it's written, so replay always gets the same result.

So the rule is: **log the call, keep side effects out of replay, and stamp anything random into the line.** You don't need to log both.

